, 288, 867, 309], \"page_id\": 128}], \"content\": \"[Source: FMS]\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 3, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [138, 314, 1486, 1115], \"page_id\": 128}], \"content\": \"<table><tr><td>Balance Oct. 1, 1999</td><td>$74,838,164</td></tr><tr><td>Revenue:</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Investment revenue:</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Interest on investments</td><td>3,637,764</td></tr><tr><td>Loss on sale of securities</td><td>-883</td></tr><tr><td>Total investment revenue</td><td>3,636,881</td></tr><tr><td>Total revenue</td><td>3,636,881</td></tr><tr><td>Nonexpenditure transfers:</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>U.S. Coast Guard</td><td>1,533,810</td></tr><tr><td>Total nonexpenditure transfers</td><td>1,533,810</td></tr><tr><td>Balance Sept. 30, 2000</td><td>$76,941,234</td></tr></table>\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 4, \"type\": \"table\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [137, 1131, 801, 1172], \"page_id\": 128}], \"content\": \"Note\\u2014The expected condition and results of operations for fiscal 2001 through 2005 were not available at the time of publication.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 5, \"type\": \"footnote\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [142, 90, 186, 123], \"page_id\": 129}], \"content\": \"132\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 0, \"type\": \"page_number\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [719, 90, 907, 123], \"page_id\": 129}], \"content\": \"TRUST FUNDS\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 1, \"type\": \"page_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [410, 193, 1220, 236], \"page_id\": 129}], \"content\": \"INTRODUCTION: Oil Spill Liability Trust Fund\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 2, \"type\": \"section_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [137, 245, 803, 441], \"page_id\": 129}], \"content\": \"The Oil Spill Liability Trust Fund was established on the books of the Treasury by section 8033 of the Omnibus Budget Reconciliation Act of 1986 (Public Law 99-509). It was made effective on January 1, 1990, by section 7811(m)(3) of the Omnibus Budget Reconciliation Act of 1989 (Public Law 101-239) and amended by section 9001 of the Oil Pollution Act of 1990 (Public Law 101-380).\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 3, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [138, 441, 803, 636], \"page_id\": 129}], \"content\": \"Amounts equiv a lent to the taxes re ceived from the en vi ron men tal tax on pe tro leum, but only to the ex tent of the Oil Spill Li a bil ity Trust Fund rate, are ap pro pri ated to the fund. The tax is not ap plied dur ing any cal en dar quar ter if the Sec re tary of the Treas ury es ti mates that, as of the close of the pre ced ing calen dar quar ter, the unob ligated bal ance in the fund exceeds $1 billion.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 4, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [140, 636, 801, 698], \"page_id\": 129}], \"content\": \"Certain amounts were transferred from other funds and wereappropriated to the Oil Spill Li a bil ity Trust Fund as pro-\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 5, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [827, 245, 1488, 328], \"page_id\": 129}], \"content\": \"vided by 26 United States Code 9509(b). Certain paid penalties and amounts recovered for damages are also appropriated to the fund.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 6, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [827, 328, 1488, 441], \"page_id\": 129}], \"content\": \"Amounts in the fund are available for oil spill cleanup costs and certain other related purposes as provided by appropriations acts or section 6002(b) of the Oil Pollution Act of 1990 (Public Law 101-380).\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 7, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [827, 441, 1488, 549], \"page_id\": 129}], \"content\": \"When the provisions of 26 United States Code 9602(b) are met, amounts available in the fund exceeding outlay requirements are invested in public debt securities. Interest is credited to the fund.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 8, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [827, 549, 1488, 698], \"page_id\": 129}], \"content\": \"An nual re ports to Con gress, re quired by 26 United States Code 9602(a), are sub mit ted by the Sec re tary of the Trea sury. These re ports are re quired to cover the fi nan cial con di tion and re sults of op er a tions of the fund dur ing the past fis cal year and those ex pected dur ing the next 5 fis cal years.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 9, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [494, 738, 1140, 806], \"page_id\": 129}], \"content\": \"TABLE TF-21.\\u2014Oil Spill Liability Trust Fund Results of Operations, Fiscal 2000\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 10, \"type\": \"caption\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [137, 832, 1490, 1784], \"page_id\": 129}], \"content\": \"<table><tr><td colspan=\\\"2\\\">[Source: FMS]</td></tr><tr><td>Balance Oct. 1, 1999</td><td>$1,027,784,314</td></tr><tr><td>Revenue:</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Tax on crude oil/pe tro leum products</td><td>5,000</td></tr><tr><td>Trans-Alaska Pipe line li a bil ity fund deposit</td><td>181,846,967</td></tr><tr><td>Cost recoveries</td><td>6,645,108</td></tr><tr><td>Fines and penalties</td><td>33,461,537</td></tr><tr><td>Total revenue before interest</td><td>221,958,612</td></tr><tr><td>Interest on investments</td><td>62,131,373</td></tr><tr><td>Realized loss</td><td>-77,896</td></tr><tr><td>Total in vestment revenue</td><td>62,053,477</td></tr><tr><td>Total revenue</td><td>284,012,089</td></tr><tr><td>Expenditures:</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Treasury admin is trative expense - Bureau of Public Debt</td><td>2,141</td></tr><tr><td>Nonexpenditure transfers:</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Trans fer to Denalia Commission</td><td>5,475,967</td></tr><tr><td>Trans fer to the De part ment of the In te rior</td><td>6,118,000</td></tr><tr><td>Trans fer to Environmental Protection Agency</td><td>14,974,400</td></tr><tr><td>Trans fer to De part ment of Transportation</td><td>5,479,000</td></tr><tr><td>Trans fer to U.S. Coast Guard</td><td>285,778,840</td></tr><tr><td>Total nonexpenditures transfers</td><td>317,826,207</td></tr><tr><td>Total expenditure/nonexpenditure transfers</td><td>317,828,348</td></tr><tr><td>Balance Sept. 30, 2000</td><td>$993,968,055</td></tr></table>\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 11, \"type\": \"table\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [138, 1806, 796, 1841], \"page_id\": 129}], \"content\": \"Note.\\u2014The expected condition and results of operations for fiscal 2001 through 2005 were not available at the time of publication.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 12, \"type\": \"footnote\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [718, 85, 909, 125], \"page_id\": 130}], \"content\": \"TRUST FUNDS\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 0, \"type\": \"page_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [1441, 87, 1494, 125], \"page_id\": 130}], \"content\": \"133\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 1, \"type\": \"page_number\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [291, 193, 1341, 238], \"page_id\": 130}], \"content\": \"INTRODUCTION: Vaccine Injury Compensation Trust Fund\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 2, \"type\": \"section_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [135, 243, 812, 441], \"page_id\": 130}], \"content\": \"The Vaccine Injury Compensation Trust Fund was created on the books of the Treasury by section 9202 of the Revenue Act of 1987 (Public Law 100-203, approved December 22, 1987). Excise taxes on diphtheria, pertussis and tetanus; diphtheria, tetanus; measles, mumps and rubella; and polio vaccines (26 United States Code 4131) are appropriated into the fund, which is a source of funds to pay claims for compensation\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 3, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [823, 243, 1492, 302], \"page_id\": 130}], \"content\": \"for vac cine-related in jury or death oc cur ring after Oc to ber 1, 1988.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 4, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [827, 302, 1494, 448], \"page_id\": 130}], \"content\": \"Annual reports to Congress, required by 26 United States Code 9602(a), are submitted by the Secretary of the Treasury. These reports are required to cover the financial condition and results of operations of the fund during the past fiscal year and those expected during the next 5 fiscal years.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 5, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [393, 644, 1239, 724], \"page_id\": 130}], \"content\": \"TABLE TF-22.\\u2014Vac cine Injury Compensation Trust Fund Results of Operations, Fiscal 2000\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 6, \"type\": \"section_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [135, 733, 1495, 1749], \"page_id\": 130}], \"content\": \"<table><tr><td colspan=\\\"2\\\">[Source: FMS]</td></tr><tr><td>Balance Oct. 1, 1999</td><td>$1,455,899,015</td></tr><tr><td>Receipts:</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Excise tax receipts</td><td>127,379,500</td></tr><tr><td>Interest on investments</td><td>74,836,515</td></tr><tr><td>Total receipts</td><td>202,216,015</td></tr><tr><td>Outlays:</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>U.S. Claims Court expense</td><td>3,413,122</td></tr><tr><td>U.S. Department of Justice expense</td><td>4,378,463</td></tr><tr><td>Total outlays</td><td>7,791,586</td></tr><tr><td>Nonexpenditure transfers:</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Trans fer to Health Resources</td><td>62,548,437</td></tr><tr><td>Total outlays/transfers</td><td>70,340,022</td></tr><tr><td>Balance Sept. 30, 2000</td><td>$1,587,775,007</td></tr></table>\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 7, \"type\": \"table\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [146, 1749, 818, 1798], \"page_id\": 130}], \"content\": \"Note.\\u2014The expected condition and results of operations for fiscal 2001 through 2005 were not available at the time of publication.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 8, \"type\": \"footnote\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [142, 87, 186, 127], \"page_id\": 131}], \"content\": \"134\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 0, \"type\": \"page_number\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [716, 90, 907, 123], \"page_id\": 131}], \"content\": \"TRUST FUNDS\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 1, \"type\": \"page_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [399, 193, 1228, 231], \"page_id\": 131}], \"content\": \"TABLE TF-23.\\u2014Na tional Rec re ational Trails Trust Fund*\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 2, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [311, 382, 1313, 453], \"page_id\": 131}], \"content\": \"*Note.\\u2014Section 9011 of the Transportation Equity Act for the 21st Century, Public Law 105-178, repealed the National Recreational Trails Trust Fund.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 3, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [678, 94, 953, 132], \"page_id\": 132}], \"content\": \"TECHNICAL PAPERS\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 0, \"type\": \"page_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [1446, 94, 1494, 132], \"page_id\": 132}], \"content\": \"135\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 1, \"type\": \"page_number\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [607, 196, 1022, 250], \"page_id\": 132}], \"content\": \"Research Paper Series\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 2, \"type\": \"title\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [239, 306, 1395, 351], \"page_id\": 132}], \"content\": \"Avail able through the Of fice of the As sis tant Sec re tary for Eco nomic Pol icy\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 3, \"type\": \"title\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [137, 382, 1362, 420], \"page_id\": 132}], \"content\": \"9002. \\\"Historical Trends in the U.S. Cost of Capital.\\\" Robert Gillingham and John S. Greenlees. December 1990.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 4, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [138, 453, 1474, 523], \"page_id\": 132}], \"content\": \"9003. \\\"The Effect of Marginal Tax Rates on Capital Gains Revenue: Another Look at the Evidence.\\\" Robert Gillingham and John S. Greenlees. December 1990.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 5, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [135, 552, 1477, 629], \"page_id\": 132}], \"content\": \"9004. \\\"An Econometric Model of Capital Gains Realization Behavior.\\\" Robert Gillingham, John S. Greenlees and Kimberly D. Zieschang August 1990.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 6, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [138, 660, 1441, 728], \"page_id\": 132}], \"content\": \"9101. \\\"The Impact of Government Deficits on Personal and National Saving Rates.\\\" (Revised) Michael R. Darby, Robert Gillingham and John S. Greenlees . February 1991.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 7, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [131, 764, 991, 799], \"page_id\": 132}], \"content\": \"9102. \\\"Social Security and the Public Debt.\\\" James E. Duggan. October 1991.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 8, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [135, 834, 1107, 872], \"page_id\": 132}], \"content\": \"9201. \\\"Issues in Eastern European Social Security Reform.\\\" John C. Hambor. June 1992.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 9, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [133, 905, 1137, 940], \"page_id\": 132}], \"content\": \"9202. \\\"Life-Health Insurance Markets.\\\" John S. Greenlees and James E. Duggan July 1992.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 10, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [137, 974, 1195, 1011], \"page_id\": 132}], \"content\": \"9203. \\\"Property-Casualty Insurance Markets.\\\" Lucy Huffman and David Bernstein. August 1992.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 11, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [138, 1042, 1450, 1115], \"page_id\": 132}], \"content\": \"9301. \\u201cThe Bank-Reported Data in the U.S. Balance of Payments: Basic Features and an Assessment of their Reliability.\\u201d Michael Cayton. February 1993.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 12, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [137, 1148, 1435, 1219], \"page_id\": 132}], \"content\": \"9302. \\\"The Returns Paid to Early Social Security Cohorts.\\\" James E. Duggan, Robert Gillingham and John S. Greenlees. April 1993.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 13, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [133, 1252, 1481, 1318], \"page_id\": 132}], \"content\": \"9303. \\\"Distributional Effects of Social Security: The Notch Issue Revisited.\\\" James E.Duggan, Robert Gillingham and John S. Greenlees. Revised April 1995.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 14, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [135, 1353, 1404, 1424], \"page_id\": 132}], \"content\": \"9501. \\u201cProgressive Returns to Social Security? An Answer from Social Security Records.\\u201d James E. Duggan, Robert Gillingham and John S. Greenlees. November 1995.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 15, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [135, 1457, 1439, 1527], \"page_id\": 132}], \"content\": \"9701. \\u201cHousing Bias in the CPI and Its Effects on the Budget Deficit and Social Security Trust Fund.\\u201d James E. Duggan, Robert Gillingham and John S. Greenlees . January 1997.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 16, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [131, 1558, 1490, 1626], \"page_id\": 132}], \"content\": \"9702. \\\"An Improved Method for Estimating the Total Taxable Resources of the States.\\\" Michael Compson and John Navratil December 1997.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 17, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [654, 1659, 998, 1780], \"page_id\": 132}], \"content\": \"Copies may be obtained by writing to: Ann Bailey, Department of the Treasury 1500 Pennsylvania Ave., NW., Room 4422 Washington, D.C. 20220\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 18, \"type\": \"page_footer\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [568, 1801, 1060, 1836], \"page_id\": 132}], \"content\": \"Telephone (202) 622-2010, or fax (202) 622-1294 or 622-2563.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 19, \"type\": \"page_footer\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [142, 99, 186, 125], \"page_id\": 133}], \"content\": \"136\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 0, \"type\": \"page_number\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [424, 200, 1208, 316], \"page_id\": 133}], \"content\": \"Glossary With References to Applicable Sections and Tables\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 1, \"type\": \"section_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [599, 328, 1025, 358], \"page_id\": 133}], \"content\": \"Source: Financial Management Service\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 2, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [142, 396, 794, 610], \"page_id\": 133}], \"content\": \"Accrued discount (SBN-1, -2, -3)\\u2014Interest that accumulates on savings bonds from the date of purchase until the date of redemption or final maturity, whichever comes first. Series A, B, C, D, E, EE, F, I and J are discount or accrual type bonds\\u2014meaning principal and interest are paid when bonds are redeemed. Series G, H, HH and K are current-income bonds, and the semi annual interest paid to their holders is not included in accrued discount.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 3, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [138, 622, 794, 759], \"page_id\": 133}], \"content\": \"Amounts outstanding and in circulation (USCC)\\u2014Includes all is sues by the Bureau of the Mint purposely in tended as a medium of ex change. Coins sold by the Bureau of the Mint at pre mium prices are ex cluded; how ever, uncirculated coin sets sold at face value plus han dling charge are in cluded.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 4, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [142, 761, 796, 874], \"page_id\": 133}], \"content\": \"Average dis count rate (PDO-2, -3)\\u2014In Trea sury bill auc tions, purchaserstendercompetitivebids on a discount rate ba- sis. The av er age dis count rate is the weighted, or ad justed, average of all bids ac cepted in the a uction.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 5, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [138, 874, 798, 1065], \"page_id\": 133}], \"content\": \"Budget authority (\\\"Federal Fiscal Operations\\\")\\u2014Congress passes laws giving budget authority to Government entities, which gives the agencies the power to spend Federal funds. Congress can stipulate various criteria for the spending of these funds. For example, Congress can stipulate that a given agency must spend within a specific year, number of years, or any time in the future.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 6, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [142, 1075, 794, 1320], \"page_id\": 133}], \"content\": \"The basic forms of budget authority are appropriations, authority to borrow, contract authority, and authority to obligate and expend offsetting receipts and collections. The period of time during which Congress makes funds available may be specified as 1-year, multi-ple-year or no-year. The available amount may be classified as either definite or indefinite; a specific amount or an unspecified amount can be made available. Authority also may be classified as current or permanent. Permanent authority requires no current action by Congress.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 7, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [142, 1325, 794, 1384], \"page_id\": 133}], \"content\": \"Budget deficit\\u2014The to tal, cu mu la tive amount by which bud-get outlays (spending) exceed budget receipts (income).\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 8, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [142, 1386, 796, 1527], \"page_id\": 133}], \"content\": \"Cash management bills (PDO-2)\\u2014Marketable Treasury bills of irregularmaturity lengths, sold periodicallyto fund short-term cash needs of Trea sury. Their sale, hav ing higher min1mum and multi ple purcha re quire ments than those of other is sues, is generally re stricted to compet itive bid ders.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 9, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [142, 1530, 801, 1669], \"page_id\": 133}], \"content\": \"Competitive tenders (\\\"Treasury Financing Operations\\\")\\u2014A bid to pur chase a stated amount of one is sue of Treac sury se curi ties at a spec i fied yield or dis count. The bid is ac cepted if it is within the range ac cepted in the auc tion. (See Noncompetitivetenders.)\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 10, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [144, 1671, 769, 1700], \"page_id\": 133}], \"content\": \"Coupon issue\\u2014The is sue of bonds or notes (pub lic debt).\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 11, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [142, 1707, 794, 1791], \"page_id\": 133}], \"content\": \"Currency no longer issued (USC)\\u2014Old and new series gold and silver certificates, Federal Reserve notes, national bank notes and 1890 Series Treasury notes.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 12, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [142, 1796, 794, 1876], \"page_id\": 133}], \"content\": \"Current income bonds (\\\"U.S. Savings Bonds and Notes\\\")\\u2014Bonds paying semi annual interest to holders. Interest is not in cluded in ac crued dis count.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 13, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [840, 396, 1494, 641], \"page_id\": 133}], \"content\": \"Debt outstanding subject to limitation (FD-6)\\u2014The debt in curried by the Treasury subject to the statutory limit set by Congress. Until World War I, a specific amount of debt was authorized to each separate security issue. Beginning with the Second Liberty Loan Act of 1917, the nature of the limitation was modified until, in 1941, it developed into an overall limit on the outstanding Federal debt. As of December 2000, the debt limit was $5,950,000 million; the limit may change from year to year.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 14, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [840, 644, 1494, 783], \"page_id\": 133}], \"content\": \"The debt subject to limitation in cludes most of Treasury's public debt except securities issued to the Federal Financing Bank, upon which there is a limitation of $15 billion, and certain categories of older debt (totaling approximately $595 million as of February 1991).\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 15, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [840, 787, 1484, 844], \"page_id\": 133}], \"content\": \"Discount\\u2014The in ter est de ducted in ad vance when pur chas-ing notes or bonds. (See Ac cru ed dis count.)\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 16, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [840, 851, 1490, 988], \"page_id\": 133}], \"content\": \"Dis count rate (PDO-2)\\u2014The difference between par value and the actual purchase price paid, an nul al ized over a 360-day year. Because this rate is less than the actual yield (coupon-equivalent rate), the yield should be used in any com par i son with coupon is sue se curi ties.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 17, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [840, 995, 1484, 1049], \"page_id\": 133}], \"content\": \"Dollar coins (USCC)\\u2014Include standardsilver and nonsilver coins.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 18, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [840, 1056, 1490, 1193], \"page_id\": 133}], \"content\": \"Domestic series (FD-2)\\u2014Nonmarketable, interest and non-interest-bearing securities issued periodically by Treasury to the Resolution Funding Corporation (RFC) for investment of funds authorized under section 21B of the Federal Home Loan Bank Act (12 United States Code 1441b).\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 19, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [840, 1200, 1488, 1306], \"page_id\": 133}], \"content\": \"Federal intrafund transactions (\\\"Federal Fiscal Operations\\\")\\u2014Intrabudgetary transactions in which payments and receipts both occur within the same Federal fund group (Federal funds or trust funds).\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 20, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [840, 1315, 1490, 1506], \"page_id\": 133}], \"content\": \"Federal Reserve notes (USCC)\\u2014Is sued by the U.S. Government to the public through the Federal Reserve Banks and their member banks. They represent money owed by the Government to the public. Currently, the item \\\"Federal Reserve notes\\u2014amounts outstanding\\\" consists of new series issues. The Federal Reserve note is the only class of currency currently is sued.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 21, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [840, 1509, 1490, 1593], \"page_id\": 133}], \"content\": \"Foreign (\\u201cForeign Currency Positions,\\u201d IFS-2, -3)\\u2014Locations other than those included under the definition of the United States. (See United States.)\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 22, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [840, 1600, 1494, 1846], \"page_id\": 133}], \"content\": \"For eigner (\\\"Capital Movements,\\\" IFS-2)\\u2014Allinstitutions andindividuals living outside the United States, including U.S. cit i zens liv ing abroad, and branches, sub sid iar ies, and other af fil i ates abroad of U.S. banks and busi ness con cerns; central gov ern ments, central banks, and other of fi cial in sti tu tions of coun tries other than the United States; and in ter na tional and regional organizations, wherever located. Also re fers to per sons in the United States to the ex tent that they are known by reportinginstitutions to beacting for foreigners.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 23, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [840, 1848, 1494, 1909], \"page_id\": 133}], \"content\": \"Foreignofficialinstitutions (\\\"CapitalMovements\\\")\\u2014Includes central governments of foreign countries, including all\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 24, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [1443, 97, 1488, 130], \"page_id\": 134}], \"content\": \"137\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 0, \"type\": \"page_number\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [732, 200, 898, 243], \"page_id\": 134}], \"content\": \"Glossary\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 1, \"type\": \"section_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [138, 273, 796, 495], \"page_id\": 134}], \"content\": \"departments and agencies of national governments; central banks, exchange authorities, and all fiscal agents of foreign national governments that undertake activities similar to those of a treasury, central bank, or stabilization fund; diplomatic and consular establishments of foreign national governments; and any international or regional organization, including subordinate and affiliate agencies, created by treaty or convention between sovereign states.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 2, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [138, 500, 792, 693], \"page_id\": 134}], \"content\": \"Foreign public borrower (\\\"Capital Movements\\\")\\u2014Includes for eign of fi cial in sti tu tions, as de fined above, the cor porations and agencies of foreign central governments, including development banks and institutions, and other agencies that are major ity-owned by the central gov ern ment or its departments; and state provincial and local governments of for eign coun tries and their de part ments and agen cies.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 3, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [138, 695, 794, 917], \"page_id\": 134}], \"content\": \"Foreign-targeted issue (PDO-1, -3)\\u2014Foreign-targeted issues were notes sold between October 1984 and February 1986 to foreign institutions, for eign branches of U.S. institutions, for eign central banks or monetary authorities, or to international organizations in which the United States held membership. Sold as companion issues, they could be converted to domestic (normal) Treasury notes with the same maturity and interest rates. Interest was paid annually.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 4, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [138, 917, 792, 976], \"page_id\": 134}], \"content\": \"Fractional coins (USCC)\\u2014Coins minted in denominations of 50, 25, and 10 cents, and minor coins (5 cents and 1 cent).\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 5, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [138, 981, 796, 1311], \"page_id\": 134}], \"content\": \"Government account series (FD-2)\\u2014Cer tain trust fund statutes re quire the Sec re tary of the Trea sury to ap ply mon ies held by these funds to ward the is su ance of non market able spe cial se cu ri ties. These se cu ri ties are sold di rectly by Trea sury to a spe cific Gov ern ment agency, trust fund or ac count. Their rate is based on an av er age of mar ket yields on out stand ing Trea- sury ob li ga tions, and they may be re deemed at the op tion of the holder. Roughly 80 per cent of these are is sued to five hold- ers: the Fed eral Old-age and Sur vi vors In sur ance Trust Fund; the civil ser vice retire ment and disa bility fund; the Fed eral Hos pi tal In sur ance Trust Fund; the mil i tary re tire ment fund; and the Un em ploy ment Trust Fund.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 6, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [138, 1318, 796, 1426], \"page_id\": 134}], \"content\": \"Interfund transactions (\\\"Federal Fiscal Operations\\\")\\u2014Trans actions in which pay ments are made from one fund group (ei ther Fed eral funds or trust funds) to a re ceipt ac-count in an other group.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 7, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [138, 1431, 796, 1568], \"page_id\": 134}], \"content\": \"International Monetary Fund (\\u201cExchange Stabilization Fund,\\u201d IFS-1)\\u2014(IMF)Established by the United Nations, the IMF promotes international trade, stability of exchange and monetary cooperation. Members are allowed to draw from the fund.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 8, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [138, 1572, 800, 1711], \"page_id\": 134}], \"content\": \"Intrabudgetary transactions (\\\"Federal Fiscal Operations\\\")\\u2014These oc cur when pay ment and re ceipt both oc cur within the bud get, or when pay ment is made from off-budget Federal en ties whose bud get authority and outlays are ex- cluded from the bud get to tals.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 9, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [138, 1718, 792, 1912], \"page_id\": 134}], \"content\": \"Ma tured non-interest-bearing debt (SBN-1, -2, -3)\\u2014The value of outstanding savings bonds and notes that have reached final maturity and no longer earn interest. Includes all Series A-D, F, G, I, J and K bonds. Series E bonds (issued between May 1941 and November 1965), Series EE (issued since January 1980), Series H (issued from June 1952 through December 1979), and savings notes is sued between May 1967\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 10, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [838, 273, 1484, 332], \"page_id\": 134}], \"content\": \"and October 1970 have a final maturity of 30 years. Series HH bonds (is sued since January 1980) mature after 20 years.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 11, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [838, 335, 1492, 474], \"page_id\": 134}], \"content\": \"Noncompetitive tenders (\\\"Treasury Financing Operations\\\")\\u2014Of fers by an in ves tor to purchase Trea sury se cu rities at the price equiv a lent to the weighted av er age dis count rate or yield of ac cepted comp etitive ten ders in a Trea sury aue tion.Noncompetitive tenders are always ac cepted in full.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 12, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [838, 478, 1492, 646], \"page_id\": 134}], \"content\": \"Off-budget Federal entities (\\\"Federal Fiscal Operations\\\")\\u2014Federally owned and controlled entities whose transactions are excluded from the bud get to tals un der pro visions of law. Their re ceipts, out lays, and sur plus or defi c it are not in cluded in bud get re ceipts, out lays or defi c its. Their bud get au thor ity is not in cluded in to tals of the bud get.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 13, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [838, 648, 1492, 735], \"page_id\": 134}], \"content\": \"Outlays (\\\"Federal Fiscal Operations\\\")\\u2014Payments on obligations in the form of cash, checks, the issuance of bonds or notes, or the maturing of interest coupons.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 14, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [838, 738, 1490, 846], \"page_id\": 134}], \"content\": \"Own foreign offices (\\\"Capital Movements\\\")\\u2014Refers to U.S. reporting institutions' parent organizations, branches and/or majority-owned subsidiaries located outside the United States.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 15, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [838, 853, 1484, 908], \"page_id\": 134}], \"content\": \"Par value\\u2014The face value of bonds or notes, in clud ing in ter est.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 16, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [838, 917, 1492, 1136], \"page_id\": 134}], \"content\": \"Quarterly financing (\\\"Treasury Financing Operations\\\")\\u2014Treasury has his tor i cally of fered pack ages of several \\\"cou pon\\\" se curity is sues on the 15th of Feb ru ary, May, Au gust and No vem ber, or on the next work ing day. These is sues currently consi st of a 3-year note, a 10-year note and a 30-year bond. Treasury some times of fers ad di tional amounts of out stand ing long-term notes or bonds, rather than sell ing new se curity is sues. (See Re opening.)\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 17, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [838, 1138, 1490, 1252], \"page_id\": 134}], \"content\": \"Receipts (\\u201cFederal Fiscal Operations\\u201d)\\u2014 Funds collected from selling land, capital or services, as well as collections from the public (budget receipts), such as taxes, fines, duties and fees.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 18, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [838, 1256, 1490, 1370], \"page_id\": 134}], \"content\": \"Reopening (PDO-3, -4)\\u2014The offer for sale of additional amounts of outstanding is sues, rather than an entirely new is sue. A reopened is sue will always have the same maturity date, CUSIP-number and interest rate as the original is sue.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 19, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [838, 1372, 1492, 1511], \"page_id\": 134}], \"content\": \"Special drawing rights (\\u201cExchange Stabi lization Fund,\\u201d IFS-1)\\u2014In ter national as sets cre ated by IMF that serve to in crease international liquidity and provide additional interna- tional reserves. SDRs may be purchased and sold among eligible holders through IMF. (See IMF.)\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 20, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [838, 1516, 1492, 1681], \"page_id\": 134}], \"content\": \"SDR allocations are the counterpart to SDRs is sued by IMF based on members' quotas in IMF. Although shown in Exchange Stabilization Fund (ESF) statements as liabilities, they must be redeemed by ESF only in the event of liquidation of, or U.S. with drawal from, the SDR de part ment of IMF or cancellation of SDRs.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 21, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [838, 1688, 1492, 1796], \"page_id\": 134}], \"content\": \"SDR certificates are issued to the Federal Reserve System against SDRs when SDRs are legalized as money. Proceeds of monetization are deposited into an ESF account at the Federal Reserve Bank of New York.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 22, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [838, 1803, 1488, 1860], \"page_id\": 134}], \"content\": \"Spot (\\u201cForeign Currency Positions\\u201d)\\u2014Due for receipt or delivery within 2 work days.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 23, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [838, 1864, 1488, 1921], \"page_id\": 134}], \"content\": \"State and local government series (FD-2)\\u2014(SLUGs) Special nonmarketable certificates, notes and bonds offered to\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 24, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [144, 99, 182, 125], \"page_id\": 135}], \"content\": \"138\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 0, \"type\": \"page_number\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [140, 276, 794, 490], \"page_id\": 135}], \"content\": \"State and local governments as a means to invest proceeds from their own tax-exempt financing. Interest rates and maturity ties comply with IRS arbitrage provisions. SLUGs are offered in both time deposit and demand deposit forms. Time deposit certificates have maturity ties of up to 1 year. Notes mature in 1 to 10 years and bonds maturity in more than 10 years. Demand deposit certificates are 1-day certificates rolled over with a rate adjustment daily.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 1, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [140, 500, 794, 662], \"page_id\": 135}], \"content\": \"Statutory debt limit (FD-6)\\u2014By Act of Congress there is a limit, either temporary or permanent, on the amount of public debt that may be outstanding. When this limit is reached, Treasury may not sell new debt is sued until Congress in creases or extends the limit. For a detailed listing of changes in the limit since 1941, see the Budget of the United States Government.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 2, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [140, 662, 608, 693], \"page_id\": 135}], \"content\": \"(See debt outstanding subject to limitation.)\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 3, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [140, 695, 794, 778], \"page_id\": 135}], \"content\": \"STRIPS (PDO-1, -3)\\u2014Separate Trading of Registered Interest and Prin ci pal Se cu ri ties. Long-term notes and bonds may be divided into principal and interest-paying components,\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 4, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [838, 276, 1490, 382], \"page_id\": 135}], \"content\": \"which may be trans ferred and sold in amounts as small as $1,000. STRIPS are sold at auc tion at a min i mum par amount, vary ing for each is sue. The amount is an arith metic function of the issue's inter estrate.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 5, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [838, 391, 1490, 471], \"page_id\": 135}], \"content\": \"Treasury bills\\u2014The shortest term Federal security (maturity dates nor mally vary ing from 3 to 12 months), they are sold at a discount.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 6, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [838, 478, 1490, 504], \"page_id\": 135}], \"content\": \"Trust fund transaction (\\\"Federal Fiscal Opera-\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 7, \"type\": \"section_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [838, 507, 1488, 563], \"page_id\": 135}], \"content\": \"tions\\\")\\u2014An intrabudgetary transaction in which both pay-ments and re ceipts oc cur within the same trust fund group.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 8, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [838, 570, 1490, 674], \"page_id\": 135}], \"content\": \"United States\\u2014Includes the 50 States, District of Columbia, Common wealth of Puerto Rico, American Samoa, Midway Islands, Virgin Islands, Wake Island and all other territories and possessions.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 9, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [838, 686, 1490, 792], \"page_id\": 135}], \"content\": \"U.S. notes (USCC)\\u2014Legal tender notes of five different issues: 1862 ($5-$1,000 notes); 1862 ($1-$2 notes); 1863 ($5-$1,000 notes); 1863 ($1-$10,000 notes); and 1901 ($10 notes).\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 10, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [734, 200, 896, 238], \"page_id\": 135}], \"content\": \"Glossary\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 11, \"type\": \"section_header\"}], \"pages\": [{\"id\": 1, \"image_uri\": null}, {\"id\": 2, \"image_uri\": null}, {\"id\": 3, \"image_uri\": null}, {\"id\": 4, \"image_uri\": null}, {\"id\": 5, \"image_uri\": null}, {\"id\": 6, \"image_uri\": null}, {\"id\": 7, \"image_uri\": null}, {\"id\": 8, \"image_uri\": null}, {\"id\": 9, \"image_uri\": null}, {\"id\": 10, \"image_uri\": null}, {\"id\": 11, \"image_uri\": null}, {\"id\": 12, \"image_uri\": null}, {\"id\": 13, \"image_uri\": null}, {\"id\": 14, \"image_uri\": null}, {\"id\": 15, \"image_uri\": null}, {\"id\": 16, \"image_uri\": null}, {\"id\": 17, \"image_uri\": null}, {\"id\": 18, \"image_uri\": null}, {\"id\": 19, \"image_uri\": null}, {\"id\": 20, \"image_uri\": null}, {\"id\": 21, \"image_uri\": null}, {\"id\": 22, \"image_uri\": null}, {\"id\": 23, \"image_uri\": nul