\"coord\": [397, 189, 1239, 278], \"page_id\": 23}], \"content\": \"INTRODUCTION: Source and Availability of the Balance in the Account of the U.S. Treasury\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 1, \"type\": \"section_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [140, 304, 803, 580], \"page_id\": 23}], \"content\": \"The Department of the Treasury's (Treasury's) operating cash is maintained in accounts with the Federal Reserve Banks (FRBs) and branches, as well as in tax and loan accounts in other financial institutions. Major information sources include FRBs, Treasury Regional Financial Centers, Internal Revenue Service Centers, Bureau of the Public Debt and various electronic systems. As the FRB accounts are depleted, funds are called in (withdrawn) from thousands of tax and loan accounts at financial institutions throughout the country.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 2, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [140, 589, 803, 834], \"page_id\": 23}], \"content\": \"Under authority of Public Law 95-147 (codified at 31 United States Code 323), Treasury implemented a program on November 2, 1978, to invest a portion of its operating cash in obligations of depositaries maintaining tax and loan accounts. Under the Treasury tax and loan (TT&L) investment program, depository financial institutions select the manner in which they will participate. Financial institutions wishing to retain funds deposited into their tax and loan accounts in inter-\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 3, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [831, 304, 1490, 578], \"page_id\": 23}], \"content\": \"est-bearing obligations can participate. The program permits Treasury to collect funds through financial institutions and to leave the funds in TT&L depositaries and in the financial communities in which they arise until Treasury needs the funds for its operations. In this way, Treasury is able to neutralize the effect of its fluctuating operations on TT&L financial institution reserves and on the economy. Likewise, those institutions wishing to remit the funds to the Treasury account at FRBs do so as collector depositaries.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 4, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [831, 589, 1490, 830], \"page_id\": 23}], \"content\": \"Deposits to tax and loan accounts occur as customers of financial institutions deposit tax payments, which the financial institutions use to purchase Government securities. In most cases, this involves a transfer of funds from a customer's account to the tax and loan account in the same financial institution. Also, Treasury can direct the FRBs to invest excess funds in tax and loan accounts directly from the Treasury account at the FRBs.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 5, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [423, 898, 1220, 974], \"page_id\": 23}], \"content\": \"TABLE UST-1.\\u2014Elements of Change in Federal Reserve and Tax and Loan Note Account Balances\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 6, \"type\": \"section_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [592, 1006, 1049, 1028], \"page_id\": 23}], \"content\": \"[In millions of dollars. Source: Financial Management Service]\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 7, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [144, 1044, 1499, 1890], \"page_id\": 23}], \"content\": \"<table><tr><th rowspan=\\\"2\\\">Fiscal year or month</th><th colspan=\\\"2\\\">Federal Reserve accounts</th><th colspan=\\\"3\\\">Credits and withdrawals</th></tr><tr><th>Received directly (1)</th><th>Received through remittance option tax and loan depositaries (2)</th><th>Withdrawals (3)</th><th>Taxes (4)</th><th>Withdrawals (transfers to Federal Reserve accounts) (5)</th></tr><tr><td>1997</td><td>4,263,210</td><td>191,134</td><td>4,454,345</td><td>775,799</td><td>776,394</td></tr><tr><td>1998</td><td>4,238,957</td><td>259,197</td><td>4,500,895</td><td>969,668</td><td>971,671</td></tr><tr><td>1999</td><td>4,893,279</td><td>259,019</td><td>5,150,608</td><td>1,048,120</td><td>1,032,230</td></t