Reported by U.S. Banks, Brokers and Dealers with Respect to Selected Countries\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 3, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [158, 405, 964, 1214], \"page_id\": 79}], \"content\": null, \"description\": null, \"id\": 4, \"type\": \"figure\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [1044, 398, 1444, 618], \"page_id\": 79}], \"content\": \"In the first nine months of 2001, U.S. banking claims on foreigners increased $45 billion, due mainly to an increase opposite Europe. In 2000, banking claims on foreigners increased $144 billion, due to strong increases in claims opposite Europe and the banking centers in the Caribbean.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 5, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [1044, 648, 1446, 943], \"page_id\": 79}], \"content\": \"U.S. banking claims on foreigners, excluding long-term securities, are concentrated in international financial centers. The data on this page show that over three-quarters of U.S. claims currently are reported opposite the United Kingdom, \\u201cAll other Europe,\\u201d and the banking centers in the Caribbean. Foreigners domiciled in Asia hold less than 10 percent of U.S. claims, down from 20 percent at the end of 1996.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 6, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [1044, 976, 1446, 1179], \"page_id\": 79}], \"content\": \"After extremely strong growth in the mid-1990s, total U.S. banking claims on foreigners leveled off in 1998 and 1999, growing at only about 5 percent per year. In 2000, growth reached 14 percent, matching the growth rate of 1997. In 2001, claims grew again at about a 5 percent pace.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 7, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [138, 1289, 1000, 1407], \"page_id\": 79}], \"content\": null, \"description\": null, \"id\": 8, \"type\": \"figure\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [138, 1433, 1000, 1857], \"page_id\": 79}], \"content\": \"<table><tr><th colspan=\\\"6\\\">[In millions of dollars. Source: Treasury I