ons and agencies' best projections of revenues and expenditures.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 5, \"type\": \"footnote\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [148, 99, 189, 127], \"page_id\": 124}], \"content\": \"126\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 0, \"type\": \"page_number\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [366, 203, 1270, 240], \"page_id\": 124}], \"content\": \"INTRODUCTION: Hazardous Substance Superfund\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 1, \"type\": \"section_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [144, 255, 798, 504], \"page_id\": 124}], \"content\": \"The Hazardous Substance Response Trust Fund was established on the books of the Treasury in fiscal 1981, in accordance with section 221 of the Hazardous Substance Response Revenue Act of 1980 [42 United States Code 9631(a), re-pealed]. The trust fund was renamed the Hazardous Substance Superfund (Superfund) and relocated in accordance with section 517 of the Superfund Amendments and Reauthorization Act of 1986 [Public Law 99-499, dated October 17, 1986 (26 United States Code 9507)].\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 2, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [148, 507, 803, 568], \"page_id\": 124}], \"content\": \"The authority to collect excise taxes on petroleum and chemicals, and an environmental tax for all corporations with\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 3, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [840, 255, 1494, 391], \"page_id\": 124}], \"content\": \"modified alternative taxable income in excess of $2 million, expired in 1995. To implement the Superfund program, amounts are appropriated from the Superfund to the Environmental Protection Agency (EPA) for programmatic and administrative expenses.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 4, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [838, 391, 1494, 535], \"page_id\": 124}], \"content\": \"An annual report to Congress by the Secretary of Treasury is required by 26 United States Code 9602(a). These reports present the financial condition of the Superfund and the results of operation for the past fiscal year, and its expected condition during the next 5 fiscal years.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 5, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [475, 585, 1164, 655], \"page_id\": 124}], \"content\": \"TABLE TF-14.\\u2014Hazardous Substance Superfund Results of Operations, Fiscal 2001\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 6, \"type\": \"section_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [765, 679, 878, 702], \"page_id\": 124}], \"content\": \"[Source: EPA]\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 7, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [142, 710, 1494, 1379], \"page_id\": 124}], \"content\": \"<table><tr><td>Balance Oct. 1, 2000</td><td>$1,262,000,000</td></tr><tr><td>Receipts:</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Crude and petroleum</td><td>2,459,000</td></tr><tr><td>Certain chemicals</td><td>32,000</td></tr><tr><td>Corporate environmental</td><td>4,000,000</td></tr><tr><td>General fund appropriation</td><td>634,000,000</td></tr><tr><td>Cost recoveries</td><td>202,000,000</td></tr><tr><td>Fines and penalties</td><td>2,100,000</td></tr><tr><td>Interest on investments</td><td>95,000,000</td></tr><tr><td>Offsetting collections</td><td>354,000,000</td></tr><tr><td>Agency for Toxic Substance and Disease Registry offsetting collections</td><td>7,000,000</td></tr><tr><td>Total receipts</td><td>1,300,591,000</td></tr><tr><td>Expenses:</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>EPA expense</td><td>1,544,374,800</td></tr><tr><td>National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration expense</td><td>2,450,000</td></tr><tr><td>Federal Emergency Management Administration expense</td><td>1,100,000</td></tr><tr><td>Agency for Toxic Substance and Disease Registry and National Institute of Environmental Health Sciences expense</td><td>82,000,000</td></tr><tr><td>Department of Interior expense</td><td>1,000,000</td></tr><tr><td>Occupational Safety and Health Administration</td><td>650,000</td></tr><tr><td>Department of Justice expense</td><td>28,437,300</td></tr><tr><td>U.S. Coast Guar