ly form an entity not formally recognized by the United States. Data for entities of the former Yugoslavia recognized as independent states by the United States are reported under \\\"Other Europe\\\" as follows: since December 1992 for Bosnia and Herzegovina, Croatia and Slovenia; and since June 1994 for the former Yugoslav Republic of Macedonia.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 6, \"type\": \"footnote\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [663, 87, 967, 137], \"page_id\": 73}], \"content\": \"CAPITAL MOVEMENTS\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 0, \"type\": \"page_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [1454, 94, 1492, 132], \"page_id\": 73}], \"content\": \"71\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 1, \"type\": \"page_number\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [166, 184, 1470, 292], \"page_id\": 73}], \"content\": \"CHART CM-A.\\u2014U.S. Liabilities to Foreigners Reported by U.S. Banks, Brokers and Dealers with Respect to Selected Countries\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 2, \"type\": \"title\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [137, 330, 1137, 1803], \"page_id\": 73}], \"content\": null, \"description\": null, \"id\": 3, \"type\": \"figure\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [1168, 361, 1499, 1417], \"page_id\": 73}], \"content\": \"In 2002, U.S. banking liabilities increased $184 billion. In 2001, banking liabilities increased $127 billion.\\nU.S. banking liabilities to foreigners, excluding long-term securities, are concentrated in international financial centers. The data on this page show that nearly one-half of U.S. banking liabilities currently is reported opposite the United Kingdom and the banking centers in the Caribbean. Foreigners domiciled in the rest of Europe and in Asia hold an additional 40 percent.\\nU.S. banking liabilities in the mid-1990s went through a growth spurt. The annual growth rate between 1993 and 1997 averaged 10 percent. From 1998 to 2000, growth slowed to about 5 percent per year, more in line with the 1989 through 1992 period. In 2001 and 2002, growth increased to abou